From 9775579b02ada50fc6e350c7e93e02fb31fcd7e8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florian Stosse <1315-Harvester57@users.noreply.git.frama-c.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 18:18:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [e-acsl] skip the '*' arguments of format directives

With -e-acsl-validate-format-strings, validate_format checked each
directive against the argument at the directive's own position. A field
width or a precision given as '*' takes an int argument before the
argument of the directive, so every directive from the first '*' on was
checked against the wrong argument:

  printf("RECORD:%.*s\n", n, buf)
  printf: directive 1 ('%.*s') expects argument of type 'char*' but the
  corresponding argument has type 'int'

The value of a '*' precision was not read either: "%.*s" required the
whole string to be NUL-terminated, while it reads at most that many
characters.

Count the arguments taken by '*' while validating the directives, check
that each of them is an int, and use the value of a '*' precision (a
negative one is taken as omitted) to validate a "%s". The values are read
from a copy of the va_list, each argument with the type given by the
format description. A numbered directive with an unnumbered '*' is
rejected as a mix of numbered and non-numbered arguments.

The index of each non-numbered directive is now also set during this
pass: a directive starting with the '0' flag was given no index when
parsed (fetch_format_argno returns early on '0'), so in "%s %05d" the
"%05d" was checked against the first argument.

All changes are below the parser, so the line numbers printed by the
runtime in the existing oracles do not move. Numbered '*' arguments
("%2$*1$d") are still rejected by the parser.

Regression test: tests/format/printf_star.c.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
